On May 5, 8:12 pm, Saptarshi Guha <saptarshi.g...@gmail.com> wrote:
> I'm surprised that that code worked.

I think there may be a misunderstanding.  To clarify: without a reduce
phase (let's call it "experiment 1": just drop the reducer), the code
will work, and the following "error" message has no impact:

R ERROR BEGIN (map.setup):
=============

bigmemory >= 4.0 is a major revision since 3.1.2; please see package
biganalytics and http://www.bigmemory.org for more information.

R ERROR END
===========

That message is, I think, indicative of bigmemory using STDERR.  If
you load the package from a regular R console, you get the same
message.  So, I think that bigmemory sends messages to STDERR or some
other destination, which Rhipe picks up & then sends to the error
logs.

However, the other issue that arises is that the code sample I gave
*doesn't* work ("experiment 2").  :)  On my setup, if the
rnorm(100000),ncol(100) is changed to, say, rnorm(100),ncol(10), then
the reduce phase works ("experiment 3").  For the example I gave, the
reduce phase fails, with this additional error (beyond the "error" of
bigmemory):

java.io.IOException: subprocess still running
::MROutput/MRErrThread failed:java.lang.RuntimeException:

Because the system may or may not be affected by hardware, you may
need to tweak the scale a little.  I've seen it work at 10x10 and
50x50 matrices, and fail at 100x100, and, for kicks, I tested up to
5000x5000.  But, for another system, it may fail at 50x50 or be fine
for larger scales.  I suspect the key is the IO exception message, but
I'm not sure what subprocess is referenced.

Suppressing messages is an interesting idea.  I tried experiment 2,
with 100x100 matrices, and it succeeds.  It's possible that the IO
message is a redherring and that the output from bigmemory is
problematic after all.  However, it's not the error per se (because
smaller scale matrices can still work), but something else.  In any
case, its output notice is unnecessary when run remotely, so I'll
suppress its loading message.

Thanks!

David

From ua5ca085497920157 at groups.google.com  Sun May 22 00:22:45 2011
From: ua5ca085497920157 at groups.google.com (Jeremiah Rounds)
Date: Sun, 22 May 2011 00:22:45 +0000
Subject: The combiner idea (reduce versus pre-reduce-post)
Message-ID: <WK62ePZWUD0.oEY2YtNN_ngJ@rhipe.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rhipe/c/WK62ePZWUD0/m/oEY2YtNN_ngJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You ever consider revisiting the decision to just make the combiner
invoke the reduce-reduce expression instead of the pre-reduce-post
sequence?

I was experimenting with creating contingency tables (cross-tabulation
tables) which is an easy combining case. I came to the conclusion that
because the combiner=TRUE statement only invokes the reduce that there
has to be a mini-pre and a mini-post in every reduce (basically
always).  Or said differently, if you need a logic in pre then you
need a new version of that logic in reduce for the combiner, and if
you have a post logic you need a new version of that post logic in the
reduce for the combiner. For example, if your pre has an accumulator
then your reduce needs an accumulator just for the combiner.

This was the code I was thinking about.  The data.frame in chunks in
the sequence file was a bunch of integers corresponding to records
from the US census.  It cross tabulates two variables.

a = list()
a$jobname = "1990 Census Cross Tabulation"
a$mapred = list()
a$mapred$rhipe_map_buff_size  = 10
a$mapred$rhipe_reduce_buff_size = 100
a$mapred$mapred.task.timeout = 0
################################################################################################
# with is some variables we want to calculate a contingency table on
################################################################################################
with = list()
with$var1 = "dOccup"
levels(with$var1) = 0:8
with$var2 = "iWWII"
levels(with$var2) = 0:1
a$mapred$with = rawToChar(serialize(with, NULL,TRUE))
################################################################################################
# maps and reduces
################################################################################################
a$map = expression(
	map = {
		with = unserialize(charToRaw(Sys.getenv("with")))
		for(i in seq_along(map.values)){
			v = map.values[[i]]
			data = list()
			levels = list()
			for(w in with){
				data[[w]] = factor(v[,w], levels = levels(w))
			}
			rhcollect(1,table(data))
		}
	}
)
a$reduce = expression(
	pre = {
		key.accum = 0
	},
	reduce = {
		combiner.accum = 0   #pre logic for combiner
		for(i in seq_along(reduce.values))
			combiner.accum = combiner.accum + reduce.values[[i]]

################################################################################################
		# are we combining or reducing?

################################################################################################

		if(is.null(key.accum))
			rhcollect(reduce.key, combiner.accum)  #post logic for combiner
		else
			key.accum = key.accum + combiner.accum

	},
	post = {
		rhcollect(reduce.key, key.accum)
	}
)
a$inout = c("sequence", "sequence")
a$ifolder = DATAFOLDER
a$ofolder = "/wsc/jrounds/censustable"
a$combiner = TRUE
mr = do.call("rhmr", a)
ex = rhex(mr, async = TRUE)
data = rhread(a$ofolder, type="seq",max=10)

